---
import PageTitle from "@/components/PageTitle.astro";
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import "@/styles/explore.css";

const meta = {
	description:
		"An interactive reconstruction of stellar population spectra from principal components. Drag a slider and watch a featureless curve turn into a real galaxy spectrum.",
	title: "Rebuilding starlight",
	ogImage: "/images/PCA.webp",
};
---

<PageLayout meta={meta}>
	<PageTitle
		title="Rebuilding starlight"
		subtitle="How much of a star's light fits in a handful of numbers?"
	/>

	<p class="explore-status" data-status hidden></p>

	<div class="demo-pca-sed" data-demo>
		{/*
			Per-band component counts, braced over the stretch of the plot each band
			covers. Drawn as SVG by the script, from the same log-wavelength mapping the
			chart uses, so the braces cannot drift away from the dashed dividers below
			them. The label is written by the script too -- a screen reader gets one
			sentence rather than six loose numbers and words.
		*/}
		<div class="demo-bands"><svg data-bands-svg role="img" aria-label=""></svg></div>

		<div class="demo-chart">
			<canvas data-canvas role="img"
				aria-label="Spectrum of a simple stellar population, reconstructed from principal components."
			></canvas>
			{/* Overlaid rather than placed below, so it reads as part of the figure. */}
			<div class="demo-legend">
				<span class="k-recon">rebuilt from components</span>
				<span class="k-truth">the real spectrum</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		<canvas class="demo-residual" data-residual role="img"
			aria-label="Reconstruction error against wavelength, as a percentage."></canvas>

		<p class="visually-hidden" data-live aria-live="polite"></p>

		<div class="demo-controls">
			<div class="demo-control">
				<label for="slider-n">Ingredients used</label>
				<input type="range" id="slider-n" data-slider-n min="3" max="130" step="1" />
				{/* One line, again: Astro trims the newline between a text node and an
				    element, which was eating the space after the middot. */}
				<p class="demo-readout"><strong data-n>12</strong> ingredients &middot; <strong data-accuracy>--</strong> of the light is right</p>
				<div class="demo-meter"><i data-bar></i></div>
			</div>

			<div class="demo-control">
				<label for="slider-age">How long ago were these stars born?</label>
				<input type="range" id="slider-age" data-slider-age min="0" max="10" step="1" />
				<p class="demo-readout"><strong data-age-label>--</strong> ago</p>
				<div class="demo-swatch-row">
					<div class="demo-swatch" aria-hidden="true"><i data-swatch></i></div>
					<p class="demo-swatch-note">
						The colour these stars would actually look to your eye, worked out from
						the rebuilt spectrum.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-why">
			<h2>Why this matters</h2>
			<p>
				A galaxy is millions of these batches of stars added together, and a
				simulated universe holds millions of galaxies. Working out the light of
				every one of them, wavelength by wavelength, is the step that makes the
				calculation unaffordable.
			</p>
			<p>
				{/* Kept on one line: Astro trims the newline between a text node and an
				    element, which silently closed the gap in "instead of 1200". */}
				Keeping <strong><span data-fill="nFor1">87</span></strong> numbers instead of <strong><span data-fill="nlam">1200</span></strong> reproduces the spectrum to better than 1 per cent &mdash; about <strong><span data-fill="ratio">14</span>&times;</strong> less to compute and store. That is the difference between a calculation you can run across a whole model universe and one you cannot.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-deep">
			<h2>The detail</h2>

			<p>
				Principal component analysis is applied to <span data-fill="nSsps">106</span> simple stellar population spectra from FSPS with a Kroupa (2001) IMF, at solar metallicity, spanning 0.1 Myr to 20 Gyr. Each spectrum is reconstructed as
			</p>

			<p class="demo-eq">
				<em>f</em>(&lambda;) = &eta;&#8202;[&#8202;<em>x̄</em>(&lambda;) + &Sigma;<sub
					>j</sub
				> &alpha;<sub>j</sub><em>v</em><sub>j</sub>(&lambda;)&#8202;]
			</p>

			<p>
				The analysis runs separately on the three wavelength bands marked above,
				and the dashed dividers on the chart are their boundaries. A single
				whole-range basis reproduces the optical almost perfectly but cannot reach
				the far ultraviolet, where an old population emits about
				10<sup>&minus;4</sup> of its peak flux. The residual panel greys out the
				region below the Lyman break, where there is so little light that a
				percentage error stops meaning anything.
			</p>

			<table class="demo-facts">
				<tr>
					<th>95th-percentile error, per band</th>
					<td data-perband>&mdash;</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Components for &lt; 5 % error</th>
					<td><span data-fill="nFor5">58</span> &mdash; the thesis reports 68</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Components for &lt; 1 % error</th>
					<td><span data-fill="nFor1">87</span> &mdash; the thesis reports 85</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Wavelength grid</th>
					<td>
						<span data-fill="nlam">1200</span> log-spaced bins over 1000&ndash;30&#8202;000 &#8491;, decimated from the FSPS native <span data-fill="nlamNative">4842</span>
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Normalisation</th>
					<td>
						L<sup>2</sup> norm, on linear flux. A logarithmic normalisation
						reconstructs better, and the thesis shows this, then rejects it: a
						composite stellar population is a <em>linear</em> superposition of these
						spectra, so a log-space basis cannot be summed and the compression buys
						nothing in the galaxy formation model it exists to serve.
					</td>
				</tr>
			</table>

			<p>
				Method and results follow the MSc thesis, <a href="https://etheses.durham.ac.uk/13823/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Application of Principal Component Analysis to Galaxy Spectral Energy Distributions</a> (Durham, 2020). The basis here is regenerated from scratch rather than taken from the original analysis, so the component counts differ slightly.
			</p>

			<div class="demo-downloads">
				<a href="/data/pca-sed/README.md">Data notes</a>
				<a href="/data/pca-sed/basis.json">basis.json</a>
				<a href="/data/pca-sed/accuracy.json">accuracy.json</a>
				<a href="/data/pca-sed/ssps.json">ssps.json</a>
				<a
					href="https://github.com/SKoonkor/skoonkor.github.io/blob/main/tools/build_pca_sed.py"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener">build script</a
				>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<noscript>
		<p>
			This page rebuilds galaxy spectra in your browser, so it needs JavaScript. The
			static version of the result is below.
		</p>
		<p>
			<img
				src="/images/PCA.webp"
				width="804"
				height="782"
				alt="Reconstructed stellar population spectra compared with the originals, with residuals."
			/>
		</p>
	</noscript>
</PageLayout>

<script>
	import { init } from "@/scripts/explore/pca-sed.js";

	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-demo]");
	if (root) void init(root);
</script>
